== Motivation ==

Bitcoin has three signature hash algorithms in use.
The original one covers bare, P2PKH, and P2SH inputs, BIP 143 covers segwit v0, and BIP 341 covers taproot.
The first two have known defects that BIP 341 does not have, and that were never fixed for the inputs that predate it.

'''Quadratic hashing (CVE-2013-2292).'''
The original algorithm reserializes the whole transaction for every input, so the cost of hashing for an n-input transaction grows with n<sup>2</sup>.
BIP 143 and BIP 341 compute their transaction-wide hashes once and do not have this defect.

'''Signing without every input amount (CVE-2020-14199).'''
BIP 143 commits to the amount of the input being signed, but not to the amounts of the other inputs.
A signing device can therefore be misled about what a transaction really spends, shown a small fee, and have its signatures from two sessions combined into a valid transaction paying a much larger one.
BIP 341 commits to every spent amount, but it is a separate algorithm, and segwit v0 inputs were never given the same commitment.

One algorithm addresses both for every script type.
Taproot needs neither fix, but using the same algorithm there leaves one message format rather than several.

Changing an existing algorithm would invalidate signatures and protocols that rely on it, so this one is opt-in per signature.
The BLAKE2b upgrade already requires every node to upgrade, which makes it a natural point to introduce it.

== Specification ==

=== Activation ===

Let H be the BLAKE2b activation height (961640 on mainnet and 150308 on testnet4, not scheduled on other networks).
The BLAKE2b proof-of-work change and its activation are specified in the separate draft BIP "BLAKE2b Proof of Work (Consensus layer)".
The rules in this document apply to every transaction in a block at height H or greater, and to no transaction in a block below H.
"The unified-sighash rules are active" below means exactly this.

=== Hash type byte ===

The following bits and base values are used:

<pre>
SIGHASH_ALL           0x01
SIGHASH_NONE          0x02
SIGHASH_SINGLE        0x03
SIGHASH_UNIFIED       0x20
SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY  0x80
</pre>

A signature uses the algorithm in this document if and only if <code>SIGHASH_UNIFIED</code> is set in its hash-type byte and the unified-sighash rules are active.
The choice is made per signature, so one script may mix opted-in and other signatures, including within one <code>OP_CHECKMULTISIG</code> or across <code>OP_CHECKSIGADD</code>.
The entire hash-type byte is committed by the signature.

For bare, P2SH, and segwit v0 inputs, the low five bits retain the legacy interpretation.
<code>SIGHASH_SINGLE</code> signs the corresponding output, <code>SIGHASH_NONE</code> signs no outputs, and any other value signs all outputs.
Unknown remaining bits are committed but otherwise ignored, as with the legacy algorithm.

For taproot and tapscript inputs, the allowed low-bit values remain those defined by BIP 341.
These are <code>SIGHASH_ALL</code>, <code>SIGHASH_NONE</code>, and <code>SIGHASH_SINGLE</code>, each optionally combined with <code>SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY</code>, and with <code>SIGHASH_UNIFIED</code> set.
Any other bit makes the signature invalid.
<code>SIGHASH_DEFAULT</code> cannot be combined with <code>SIGHASH_UNIFIED</code>, so a 64-byte Schnorr signature never opts in and an opted-in one is always 65 bytes.
The accepted opted-in taproot hash types are therefore exactly <code>0x21</code>, <code>0x22</code>, <code>0x23</code>, <code>0xa1</code>, <code>0xa2</code>, and <code>0xa3</code>.

Before activation, <code>SIGHASH_UNIFIED</code> is not a defined consensus mode.
For bare, P2SH, and segwit v0 spends, a signature whose hash-type byte carries bit <code>0x20</code> can still be valid before activation if it was created for the pre-existing legacy or segwit v0 message.
It does not opt in to the unified message until the unified-sighash rules are active.
For taproot and tapscript spends, such a signature is invalid before activation, because BIP 341 already refuses hash types it does not define.

After activation, every signature whose hash-type byte carries bit <code>0x20</code> is verified under this document only.
A bare, P2SH, or segwit v0 signature that carries the bit but was made over the pre-existing message therefore stops being valid at height H.

Opting in changes only the message that is hashed.
Signature encoding rules, the BIP 341 signature size rules, sigop counting, BIP 342 validation weight, and every other script rule are unchanged.

=== Message ===

Let <code>spent_outputs</code> be the outputs spent by the transaction's inputs, in input order.

Define these component hashes as single SHA256 hashes over the concatenation of the listed serializations:

<pre>
sha_prevouts   = SHA256(for each input: outpoint)
sha_amounts    = SHA256(for each spent output: amount)
sha_scripts    = SHA256(for each spent output: scriptPubKey)
sha_sequences  = SHA256(for each input: nSequence)
sha_outputs    = SHA256(for each output: amount, scriptPubKey)
</pre>

These are computed over all inputs and outputs of the transaction, whatever the script types of its inputs and whether or not it has witness data.
A verifier therefore needs every spent output of a transaction to check any opted-in signature in it, including one on a bare input.

Amounts are 8-byte signed little-endian integers.
Outpoints are serialized as 32-byte transaction identifiers followed by 4-byte little-endian output indexes.
ScriptPubKeys are serialized as compact-size length followed by bytes.
A serialized output is its amount followed by its scriptPubKey, both as above.

The signature hash is:

<pre>
TaggedHash("UnifiedSighash", message)
</pre>

using the BIP 340 tagged-hash construction.
The message is laid out in the same order as the BIP 341 message, so the two can be read side by side:

<pre>
1 byte    epoch, 0
1 byte    hash type
4 bytes   transaction version, little endian
5 bytes   transaction locktime, little endian (4 bytes today, then one zero byte)

if SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY is not set:
32 bytes  sha_prevouts
32 bytes  sha_amounts
32 bytes  sha_scripts
32 bytes  sha_sequences

if low five bits are neither SIGHASH_NONE nor SIGHASH_SINGLE:
32 bytes  sha_outputs

1 byte    script type (where BIP 341 writes its spend type)

if SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY is set:
36 bytes  this input's outpoint
variable  this input's spent output (amount, scriptPubKey)
4 bytes   this input's nSequence
else:
4 bytes   this input's index, little endian

then a script-type-specific middle (scriptCode or annex, below)

if low five bits are SIGHASH_SINGLE:
32 bytes  SHA256(serialized output at the input index)

if script type is 3 (tapscript):
32 bytes  tapleaf hash
1 byte    key version, 0
4 bytes   codeseparator position, little endian, or 0xffffffff if none
</pre>

The epoch byte is BIP 341's.
It is zero and gives a later revision the same room to move that BIP 341 left itself.

The hash type is committed as the single byte the signature carries, exactly as BIP 341 writes it.
Consensus reads the hash type from the last byte of the signature, so a wider field could hold a value no verifier would ever reconstruct.

The locktime is committed as five bytes rather than the four it occupies in today's transaction serialization.
Four bytes run out on 2106-02-07, and a later protocol change widening the field would otherwise have to change this message and invalidate every signature made under it.
The fifth byte is zero until something sets it.

The script type byte is:

{| class="wikitable"
! Value
! Meaning
|-
| 0 || bare or P2SH
|-
| 1 || segwit v0, native or nested in P2SH
|-
| 2 || taproot key path
|-
| 3 || tapscript
|}

For script types 0 and 1, the script-type-specific middle is the scriptCode serialized as a compact-size length followed by bytes.
The scriptCode is the script the pre-existing algorithms already use, the script in which the signature opcode executes.
That is normally the scriptPubKey for a bare input, the redeemScript for P2SH, the witnessScript for P2WSH, and for P2WPKH the implied P2PKH script as in BIP 143.
It runs from just after the most recently executed <code>OP_CODESEPARATOR</code>, or from the start of the executing script if none has executed, to the end of that script.
For script type 1 this is the BIP 143 scriptCode.
For script type 0, the signature arguments of the executing opcode are then removed from it with the legacy procedure.
It deletes, at opcode boundaries, each occurrence of the signature encoded as a single data push, which is <code>OP_0</code> for an empty signature and otherwise the shortest direct push or <code>OP_PUSHDATA</code> that fits its length.
That is one signature for <code>OP_CHECKSIG</code> and <code>OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY</code>, and all of them for <code>OP_CHECKMULTISIG</code> and <code>OP_CHECKMULTISIGVERIFY</code>, removed before any of them is checked.
Unlike the legacy serialization, any <code>OP_CODESEPARATOR</code> opcodes left in the scriptCode are not removed, so the scriptCode is committed byte for byte.

For script types 2 and 3, the middle is instead:

<pre>
1 byte    1 if an annex is present, otherwise 0
32 bytes  SHA256(compact-size length of the annex || annex), if an annex is present
</pre>

The annex is the one BIP 341 defines, including its leading <code>0x50</code> byte, and is hashed exactly as BIP 341 hashes it.

The tapleaf hash and the codeseparator position are those BIP 342 defines.

=== SIGHASH_SINGLE without a matching output ===

If the low five bits are <code>SIGHASH_SINGLE</code> and the input index is not less than the number of outputs, the message is undefined, and the legacy substitute value of 1 is never used.
For script types 0 and 1 the signature check then evaluates to false, exactly as for any other signature that does not verify, and script execution continues.
For script types 2 and 3 the script fails, as BIP 341 requires for a hash type it refuses.

=== Differences from the pre-existing algorithms ===

These apply only to signatures that opt in.

* <code>SIGHASH_SINGLE</code> without a matching output never yields a signable message, as described above.
* The script type byte separates the four script types, so a signature made for one is never valid for another.
* For script type 0 the scriptCode is committed byte for byte. <code>OP_CODESEPARATOR</code> opcodes left in it are not stripped as the original algorithm strips them.
* When <code>SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY</code> is not set, <code>SIGHASH_NONE</code> and <code>SIGHASH_SINGLE</code> still commit to every input's nSequence through <code>sha_sequences</code>, as BIP 341 does. The original algorithm zeroes the other inputs' sequence numbers and BIP 143 omits them, so a protocol that relies on a counterparty changing nSequence under those types does not carry over.
* Under <code>SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY</code> the input's position is not committed, so the input can still be moved into another transaction, and its own outpoint, spent output, and nSequence are committed directly. <code>SIGHASH_SINGLE|SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY</code> therefore binds the input to the output at its own index without binding either to a position. The original algorithm binds the position for script type 0 as a side effect of how it serializes outputs. BIP 341 does not, and neither does this.

=== Signing ===

The message commits to the amount and scriptPubKey of every spent output, for every script type and unless <code>SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY</code> is set.
A signer therefore needs every input's spent output, including for bare and P2SH inputs, which have never needed amounts before.
PSBT already carries them per input.
How PSBT fields record the hash type is outside the scope of this document.

Implementing the message does not require implementing BIP 341.
Signatures over it are made and verified exactly as they are today for the script type in question, so script types 0 and 1 use ECDSA as before, with no Schnorr signatures, x-only keys, or key tweaking.
Compared with BIP 143, a signer for those types needs the aggregate hashes with a single SHA256 rather than a double one, the two aggregates over every spent amount and scriptPubKey, and the BIP 340 tagged hash.

== Deployment ==

The consensus rules take effect at height H as specified above.

The deployment is scheduled on a network when that network's parameters specify an activation height.
Relay policy is keyed on the deployment being scheduled, not on the height having been reached.
As soon as it is scheduled, the mempool accepts opted-in signatures under this algorithm and refuses legacy signatures whose hash type carries the bit without opting in.
Relay accepts opted-in ECDSA signatures only with hash types <code>0x21</code>, <code>0x22</code>, <code>0x23</code>, <code>0xa1</code>, <code>0xa2</code>, and <code>0xa3</code>.
The other bytes are valid at consensus but not relayed.
On a network where the deployment is not scheduled, relay refuses any ECDSA signature whose hash type carries the bit.

Because relay accepts opted-in signatures as soon as the deployment is scheduled, a node below the activation height can hold transactions in its mempool that the next block cannot include.
This is the normal state between scheduling and height H, and also follows a reorganization back below the height.
Unlike other script rules, this one makes some signatures valid and others invalid, so passing relay policy does not imply validity in the next block below H.
Implementations should refuse a block template request in that state explicitly rather than fail on assembly.

''Note, not normative.'' The reference implementation does not yet refuse explicitly, and fails template assembly instead while it holds such a transaction below the height.

== Rationale ==

'''Why is the new algorithm opt-in?'''

The algorithm remains opt-in because old signatures and old protocols may rely on the historical behavior of <code>SIGHASH_NONE</code>, <code>SIGHASH_SINGLE</code>, and <code>SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY</code>.
Keeping the old algorithms available avoids unnecessarily invalidating those protocols.

'''Why commit to the script type and every spent output?'''

The explicit script type byte keeps a signature made for one script type from being valid for another with an otherwise identical message.
Committing to every spent output brings pre-taproot signing up to the data-commitment level expected by BIP 341 and removes the signing oracle ambiguity described by CVE-2020-14199 for opted-in inputs.
The aggregate hashes are computed once per transaction, so hashing for an opted-in signature costs a fixed amount plus its scriptCode rather than the whole transaction, which removes the quadratic cost of CVE-2013-2292 for that signature.
It does not bound worst-case validation cost, since a transaction that does not opt in still hashes quadratically.

'''Why the tag "UnifiedSighash"?'''

It names the algorithm, following BIP 341's <code>"TapSighash"</code>, and keeps this message distinct from every other BIP 340 tagged hash.
It cannot change after deployment without invalidating every signature made under it.

'''Why one algorithm for every script type, including taproot?'''

A single message format is one thing to specify, implement, test, and review, and one place for a future defect to be fixed.
The cost is that a BIP 341 signer cannot opt in by setting a bit, and has to implement this message.
Taproot keeps the parts of its message that carry meaning the others do not, the annex, the tapleaf hash, and the codeseparator position.
Opted-in taproot signatures give up <code>SIGHASH_DEFAULT</code> and its one-byte saving, since the bit needs a byte to live in.

'''Why activate at the BLAKE2b height rather than an offset from it?'''

Activating both at one height leaves no window in which blocks are mined under the new proof of work while opting in is unavailable.

'''What does a node without these rules do with an opted-in signature?'''

It computes a pre-existing message for the same byte, so the signature does not verify there.
A signature without the bit is valid under both rule sets.

== Backward compatibility ==

This rule change is not backward compatible.
Upgraded nodes accept signatures with <code>SIGHASH_UNIFIED</code> after activation if they verify under this document.
Nodes that do not implement it do not share the same post-activation consensus rules.

It also tightens an existing rule.
A pre-signed transaction with a bare, P2SH, or segwit v0 signature whose hash type carries bit <code>0x20</code>, made over the pre-existing message, cannot be included in a block at or after height H.
Such signatures are non-standard.
A scan of every mainnet block below height 961640 finds 17 confirmed signatures carrying the bit, six with hash type <code>0x21</code> and eleven with <code>0x65</code>, all between heights 505057 and 509793 in early 2018 and none since.
A transaction signed this way and held unbroadcast cannot be counted from the chain.
Where the key still exists, signing again without the bit is always possible.
If the key was destroyed after signing, as with some pre-signed refund or inheritance arrangements, the output cannot be spent.
Taproot is unaffected, since BIP 341 already makes undefined hash types invalid.

== Test vectors ==

The immutable reference vectors are in [https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin/blob/8c85b1585dac23f964e2dd32045624de7f02aa58/src/test/data/unified_sighash.json the v29.4.1.knots20260508 test-vector file].
The file is a JSON array whose first element is a header row.
Each further record specifies the <code>scriptCode</code>, raw transaction, input index, hash type, script type, spent outputs, and expected signature hash.

* The <code>scriptCode</code> is hashed as given. For script types 0 and 1 it is the final committed scriptCode, so codeseparator truncation and signature removal must not be applied to it again.
* The raw transaction is the serialization without witness data.
* The input index and hash type are integers, and the hash type is the full byte including <code>SIGHASH_UNIFIED</code>.
* The script type is a value from this document's script-type table.
* Spent outputs are <code>[amount, scriptPubKey hex]</code> pairs in input order.
* The expected signature hash is the raw 32-byte tagged-hash output in hex, not byte-reversed.
* No vector has an annex.
* For script type 2 the <code>scriptCode</code> field is unused.
* For script type 3 the tapleaf hash is computed over the <code>scriptCode</code> field with leaf version <code>0xc0</code>, and the codeseparator position is <code>0xffffffff</code>.

The reference vectors contain no <code>OP_CODESEPARATOR</code> in a scriptCode, no annex, no other codeseparator position, and no case that must fail.
[[bip-unified-sighash/unified_sighash_supplementary.json|Supplementary vectors]] cover those.
Each of their records adds a note first and an annex (hex or null) and a codeseparator position before the expected hash, and an expected hash of null means the message is undefined and the signature cannot be valid.

Implementations MUST reproduce each expected hash for the specified input data, and MUST refuse every record whose expected hash is null.

== Reference implementation ==

The reference implementation is Bitcoin Knots tag [https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin/commit/8c85b1585dac23f964e2dd32045624de7f02aa58 v29.4.1.knots20260508], principally <code>src/script/interpreter.cpp</code>, <code>src/script/interpreter.h</code>, <code>src/validation.cpp</code>, <code>src/test/sighash_tests.cpp</code>, and <code>src/test/data/unified_sighash.json</code>.
The unified signature hash code in these files is unchanged through v29.5.knots20260508.

